I mean, Eden's only been Prime Minister for a year. It could have happened under his predecessor Churchill, or it could have happened under Clement Attlee, let's say, who was the Labour leader, you know, in the late, the prime minister in the late 40s up to 1951. What if it had? Churchill, who loved the empire, who hated anti-colonial kind of nationalists, who loved mad military adventures and amphibious landings and escapades in the Mediterranean. Would Churchill have done something mad at Suez? Unquestionably he would. I mean, there's no way Churchill's walking away from this. Even Atley, though.

129
01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:23.000
I mean, Atley, who of course fought at Gallipoli. Atley is very patriotic. He always puts Britain's interests first. He went into the Korean War. Wherever possible, Atley tried to maintain the British Empire. I mean, he couldn't in India. But by and large, he's an imperialist. Would they have done anything different?

130
01:00:23.000 --> 01:00:47.000
I think they would have probably done the same, actually. So this isn't just on Eden and his errors. It's almost an inevitability of Britain's foreign policy, I think. They're going to do something misguided here. And it's telling that almost all of Eden's cabinet agree with him. So the next day, they have a cabinet meeting. They agree that they're absolutely clear. There's no legal basis to challenge NASA's decision.

131
01:00:47.000 --> 01:01:28.000
But they say our essential interests in this area must, if necessary, be safeguarded by military action. and the necessary preparations must be made. Failure to hold the Suez Canal would inevitably lead to the loss one by one of all our interests and assets in the Middle East. But it is interesting that this is all being framed in terms of foreign policy and realpolitik. There is no appeal to a kind of moral dimension. This is not about standing up to fascism or communism or whateverism they want to stand, they think, pretend they're standing up to. Well, later, when they sell the operation, they will sell it to standing up to fashion. Yeah, but what I'm asking is, is this genuine?

132
01:01:28.000 --> 01:02:00.000
Because, or is it something, you know, is it a disguise? Is it something that they're putting up to justify what they want to do out of raw dictates of power? Can't it be both? To make the analogy with the parallel that you implicitly invoked, you know, a few minutes ago, the invasion of Iraq in 2003. Did Tony Blair go along with that because he thought it was in Britain's interests to be close to America and so on, so forth? or because he thought Saddam Hussein was the new Adolf Hitler or something.

133
01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:18.000
Yeah, it's both. But when Blair made the case, he was always talking about the moral case. There seems to be a complete lack of that in the early stages of the Suez operation. I'm surprised by that. I had assumed that they would have been more about it. Maybe they're less moralistic. They're less given to moralistic stuff behind the scenes. I don't know.

134
01:02:18.000 --> 01:02:44.000
They're World War II veterans. They're a bit more world-weary and it's not their style, I guess. I guess I just thought that the Mussolini analogy was so important to Eden but it does seem to be completely lacking so far in anything that's been said. In private I guess, although he's writing it to Eisenhower all the time and I think he believes it. Anyway, it's important to say they are definitely in tune with the sort of political class in Britain and probably with the British public.

135
01:02:44.000 --> 01:03:12.000
So I think to most people in Britain when they hear this news, they say what? This tin pot Egyptian colonel seizing the Suez Canal, that's absolutely unthinkable. The Suez Canal is British and this is absolutely disgraceful. And when you look at the, now, the Munich analogy, you look at the press, the Times, so this was a hinge of history. It's like Hitler's march into the Rhineland. The telegraph, it's as if we're back in Hitler's and Mussolini's Day. And this is, the labor, the labor papers are just the same, the Daily Herald.

136
01:03:12.000 --> 01:03:35.000
No more Adolf Hitler's. There is no room for appeasement. And the mirror at this point was the single best-selling daily newspaper in the world. and the mirror issued this madly blood-curdling threat to NASA. This is what we say to Colonel Nasser. Remember Mussolini? Mussolini ended up hanging upside down by his feet in a square in Milan. Remember Adolf Hitler?

137
01:03:35.000 --> 01:04:12.000
He ended up burning in a petrol-soaked blanket outside his bunker in the heart of devastated Berlin. So, I mean, they're really leaning in to the sort of Hitler and Mussolini parallels. Hugh Gateskill, the Labour leader, in the House of Commons a few days later. 2nd of August, we are locked in a struggle for the master of the Middle East. He says, NASA's tactics are exactly the same that we encountered from Mussolini and Hitler in the years before the war. Exactly the same. They're literally the same. And there's one thing that Gatescold does say, however, which is telling and perhaps a clue to what's going to happen next.

138
01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:46.000
Gatesclays says, you know, we can't rule out the use of force. However, we must not allow ourselves to get into a position where we might be denounced in the United Nations security, councillors, aggressors, or where the majority of the Assembly were against us. And the thing is, 2006, I don't think people care that much about the United Nations. In 1956, people care a lot about the United Nations. They're absolutely obsessed with the United Nations. And so the legality or otherwise of what you're doing and a vote in the UN is seen as massively important.

139
01:04:46.000 --> 01:05:05.000
Well, because the British have helped set it up. Help set it up, exactly. So you've got to behave well. So this question of allies is crucial. So right from the start, Eden's cabinet say, well, look, if we're going to do anything, we've got to do it with two people in particular, the French and the Americans. Now, why the French? The French built the canal.

140
01:05:05.000 --> 01:05:26.000
The company is based in Paris. The directors of the company of French. But apart from Britain, most of the other shareholders are French private investors. And actually, the French right from the start, are on the same page. The French foreign minister, Christian Pino, he says, this is totally intolerable from NASA. We're going to stand up to this. France will always defend its rights and its interests.

141
01:05:26.000 --> 01:05:51.000
Well, the French also have a kind of stake in North Africa, don't they? Because they're busy fighting to keep Algeria as part, I mean, not part of the empire, but literally as part of France. As we discussed in the first half, you're always working. You don't relax. Even when you're relaxing, you're working. And so for relaxation, you went to see a film about de Gaulle in order to inform yourself about this important issue. Am I wrong? No.

142
01:05:51.000 --> 01:06:05.000
So... I love de Gaulle. What can I say? Yeah. De Gaulle in Leclerc. My very first piece of research I ever did was on French colonial Algeria. My undergraduate thesis, you're all over it. I'm all over it.

143
01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:35.000
The French have, for two years, have been fighting this war in Algeria. And again, some of this may be surprised to hear that at this point, Algeria was an integral part of France. It wasn't a colony. It was actually part of the French Republic. So in the film, they're always saying to DeGal when he leaves London on a plane for high-level meetings in Algeria, you will soon be setting foot on French soil. Yeah. So there are 10 million people in Algeria. A million of them are European.

144
01:06:35.000 --> 01:06:58.000
Nine million of them are Arabs and Berbers. This has turned by 1956 into this horrendous kind of guerrilla struggle. There are massacres in villages. There are attacks on French patrols. There are bombs going off in the centre of Algeria. these scenes that you see in the great film, the Battle of Algiers. The French have now poured in hundreds of thousands of troops. They're carrying out very brutal reprisals.

145
01:06:58.000 --> 01:07:27.000
They are torturing Algerian, sort of terrorist suspects and whatnot. Incredibly dirty war, which they are not winning. And the French have come to believe that NASA is behind all this, that NASA, as a sort of Blofeld-style agent working with the Soviet Union, is controlling the whole thing. I mean, it's interesting, isn't it, that the Western, powers seem to have brought into the myth of NASA's greatness, at least as much as the Arab powers.

146
01:07:27.000 --> 01:07:55.000
Completely. Yeah. And the bad thing is that NASA's only been, I mean, he's only been a factor in Egyptian politics for four years. But they're attributing to him sort of the powers of a great criminal mastermind or something. I suppose because they have just come out of a war in which Titanic world leaders played, an oversized part. I mean, I suppose also we've been talking about, you know, the inexorable collapse of the

147
01:07:55.000 --> 01:08:22.000
European empires, which if you're growing up must have seemed so solid and secure, and now they're just melting away. And you think there must, you need to put a human face, perhaps, to what is a much broader kind of impersonal process of change. I think that's dead right. I think you personalise it and therefore you explain it. It's almost like, it's almost like the equivalent of reaching for a conspiracy theory to explain some great economic change that you don't understand.

148
01:08:22.000 --> 01:09:10.000
So to be fair to the French, NASA is a big supporter of the FLN, which is the Algerian kind of nationalist group. NASA hosts their government in exile in Cairo. He hosts a radio station for them in Cairo, and he is supplying them via Libya with weapons and with money. And the French have got it into their heads that if they destroy NASA, they can win this war. So Pino, the foreign minister at one point told the Americans, with one successful battle in Egypt would be worth 10 in Algeria, which is rubbish and shows how they're misunderstanding the situation in Algeria. But we'll come back to the French. Let's turn now to the Americans because I mentioned he was talking to the Americans. So the bloke in the White House, we've mentioned him a bit already just in passing.

149
01:09:10.000 --> 01:09:51.000
He is the former Allied military supremo Dwight D. Eisenhower, who's 65 years old and he's a Republican. And Eisenhower cultivates this image of a kind of lovable old uncle, this kind of bumbling golfer. But in reality, you know, as anyone who worked with him in the Second World War knows, Eisenhower's very shrewd, he's methodical, he's actually a pretty ruthless political operator. He's going to be running for re-election in November, and he wants to go to the American people and say, you've got your massive cars and fridges, you know, you've never been happier. I've brought you stability, no international drama. I ended the Korean War, which is what he promised to do. He ended it straight away.

150
01:09:51.000 --> 01:10:31.000
I haven't dragged you into any more wars. The last thing Eisenhower wants to do is to drag the American people, to find himself dragged into a new war in the Middle East, especially on the side of the imperial powers. You know, they want to maybe supply the imperial powers, but they don't want to be completely identified with them. As early as the 31st of July, he got a report from a State Department representative in London who said, we believe the British government has decided to drive NASA out of Egypt. Eisenhower was horrified by that. The CIA told him, if the British and French attack NASA, it would drive the Arab world into the arms of the Soviet Union. And so from the very beginning, Eisenhower is sending signals to London.

151
01:10:31.000 --> 01:10:58.000
Please don't do anything reckless. You know, take this calmly. Think about this. And in fact, he sends his secretary of state John Foster Dulles to London to talk to Eden personally. Dulles, people may know the airport in Washington, D.C. It's a fairly useless airport. Dulles was a Republican lawyer. He was like a Washington insider. He was a very, very austere man, the son of a Presbyterian minister.

152
01:10:58.000 --> 01:11:30.000
He's a Cold War hawk. He's very intense. He's kind of very puritanical. He's not a great laugh. Dulles arrives in London on the 1st of August, and he gives Eden a letter from Eisenhower. And Eisenhower says, you know, dear Anthony, listen, I completely recognize the transcendent worth of the canal to the free world. But please do not take military action until every other, you know, peaceful form of negotiation has been exhausted. This is not what Anthony Eaton wants to hear.

153
01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:56.000
He replies four days later. He says, no, no, no. Our long-term aim is not just to get NASA to disgorge his spoils. but to replace him with a regime less hostile to the West. And here we have the very familiar analogy again. NASA has embarked on a course which is unpleasantly familiar, says Eden. His ambitions stretch from Morocco to the Persian Gulf. The parallel with Mussolini is close.

154
01:11:56.000 --> 01:12:14.000
Neither of us can forget the lives and treasure he cost us before he was finally dealt with. And then Eden goes on to say, The British people are grimly determined that NASA shall not get away with it this time because they're convinced that if he does, their existence will be at his mercy. So am I. I mean, that's ridiculous.

155
01:12:14.000 --> 01:12:29.000
Of course it's ridiculous. It's absolutely insane. It's completely ridiculous. Aside from anything else, Hitler was in command of the most intimidating war machine in Europe. NASA really isn't. I mean, the Egyptian forces are rubbish. Right, exactly.

156
01:12:29.000 --> 01:12:49.000
The Egyptian army is pretty rubbish. Egypt is not an economic superpower. Also, NASA is a sort of nationalist officer who's very got kind of matinee idle looks who likes slagging off the British and does have ambitions to kind of meddle in the politics to the Arab world, he's a pan-Arabist, all of that.

157
01:12:49.000 --> 01:13:07.000
There was absolutely no way that NASA is going to try and build an empire that stretches from Morocco to Iran. Do you think there's some moustache envy going on? There must be. And actually talking moustaches, I mean, it does remind me so much of the way that people talked about Saddam Hussein in 2003. Saddam Hussein, if not stopped, will take

158
01:13:07.000 --> 01:13:31.000
over the whole Middle East. This is the same sort of babble. So three days after this, so the 8th of August, Eden decides he needs to rally public opinion. He goes on British TV to speak to the nation. Now remember, in 1956, this is a very unusual thing to do. Most people have only just bought a TV. So the Prime Minister addressing you on TV is like a big deal.

159
01:13:31.000 --> 01:13:52.000
And, you know, Eden plays all his cards. We know this is how fascist governments behave. We remember only too well. what the cost can be of giving into fascism. If Colonel NASA's action was to succeed each one of us were to be at the mercy of one man for the supplies on which we live, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. But the thing is, because Eden made his name

160
01:13:52.000 --> 01:14:07.000
as a critic of the dictators, as a sort of enemy of the dictators, a critic of appeasement, this sounds very powerful coming from him. You know, if you're somebody in Britain, you don't really follow politics. You're like, well, Eden was right before. Why wouldn't he be right now?

161
01:14:07.000 --> 01:14:34.000
You know, there was one villain with a mustache 20 years ago, and we know what bad man he turned out to be. Here's another. So he's given his broadcast. He's written back to Eisenhower, and he's been quietly making his military preparations. So since the beginning of August, the government has been calling up military reserves. They've been requisitioning merchant ships as troop transports. They've been moving fighter planes and bombers to Cyprus and Malta. And they have a plan.

162
01:14:34.000 --> 01:15:02.000
The plan is called Operation Musketeer. And basically they're going to land royal marines to take Port Said on the north and then an airborne assault by paratroopers to take out the canal zone. And some of Eden's ministers make a very good point. If we do this, we'll just be back where we were two years ago when we had the canal zone base. We'll be back with the canal surrounded by people who hate us. And they say, instead of saying the obvious thing,

163
01:15:02.000 --> 01:15:24.000
which is maybe not do it at all, they say, They say, let's think bigger. Let's go for a full regime change. So the Chancellor, Harold McMillan, will talk about him a bit more in the next episode. Says to the Cabinet on 7th of August, our goal should be to bring about the fall of NASA and create a government in Egypt

164
01:15:24.000 --> 01:15:45.000
which will work satisfactorily with ourselves and with the other great powers. So they change the plan. The plan is now becoming something like the plan of a board game, I think, or a computer game or something. They say we'll start off with a massive air assault on Egypt, we'll land 80,000 troops at Alexandria, will seize Alexandria, and then we'll march on Cairo.

165
01:15:45.000 --> 01:16:01.000
Cairo. Yeah, I mean, I think when you're marching on Cairo, I mean, this is Napoleon. And it ended disastrously for Napoleon. There's no reason for them to believe that they're going to do any better than Napoleon. But I suppose they've been the imperial power in Egypt for basically decades and decades.

166
01:16:01.000 --> 01:16:24.000
And the habit of that is hard to shed, I guess. It's the habit of that, plus the thing that people always tell themselves, the Egyptian people will rise up and join us. They chafe under the jackboot of the dictator. I mean, we'll see how that turns out. And then they change the plan again. Actually, yeah, landing in Alexandria and trying to capture Cairo is mad. We'll keep the air attack.

167
01:16:24.000 --> 01:16:45.000
We'll smash NASA's Air Force. We'll break Egypt's economy, and then we'll land and we'll recapture the canal. And then we'll just see where we are. You know, maybe NASA will fall by some strange means. You know, who knows. Now, all the time, they're waiting for NASA to give them a pretext. They really need a pretext because what NASA's done is not illegal. The great problem, the Egyptians actually really good at running the canal.

168
01:16:45.000 --> 01:17:04.000
There's absolutely no problem at all. The canal doesn't close. I mean, how hard can it be to run a canal, frankly? You know, just keep it open. So the traffic continues as normal. This is a big problem for Eden because the longer this goes on, the more likely people are to lose interest. And in fact, the polls show the British public are losing interest. And in America, Eisenhower.

169
01:17:04.000 --> 01:17:34.000
Dulles are not budging, they're just not really interested in going in at all. So what Eden is doing is while his chiefs are drawing up the plan, he's keeping up this, he's hoping something's going to turn up and he's keeping up a pretense of wanting a negotiated settlement. So he hosts two conferences in London about the future of the canal. NASA refuses to turn up. So eventually the British decide what they're going to do, is they're going to send the Australian Prime Minister Sir Robert Menzies to Cairo to speak to NASA.

170
01:17:34.000 --> 01:18:11.000
directly. And this is a very strange visit from Sir Robert Menzies. When he arrives in Cairo, he tries to charm Nassau by doing impressions of Churchill to him. Maybe he knew that NASA had read Churchill's book. So he does that and that's the sort of, you know, him being his most charming, Australian charm. And then he administers the stick. He says, come on, mate, you know that you're only Egyptian. You're not going to be able to run the canal without Western help. You need a person to run the canal for you can't do it yourselves. NASA is outraged by this. Not unreasonably.

171
01:18:11.000 --> 01:18:35.000
NASA says afterwards, when Menz's has left, NASA says, I'm absolutely disgusted. The British have sent this Australian mule to threaten me. And he also complained, which I found very entertaining. He said he couldn't stand to Robert Menzies because he had a very sharp voice, which is the way that people often react I find to Australian visitors. He ends up warden of the sinker ports, doesn't he?

172
01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:52.000
He does. He was massively pro-British, wasn't he? Robert Menz's, I think. Anyway, he's, yeah, that hasn't gone well. Anyway, the talks get nowhere. By the end of September, Britain and France, refer the Suez dispute to the UN Security Council. Now, it's important to say this is a massive sham.

173
01:18:52.000 --> 01:19:18.000
The French foreign minister, Pino, he said to the Britain's ambassador in Paris, yeah, we're going to get the security council to discuss this, and it is window dressing. these are his words, because my government is determined, we're going to get rid of NASA anyway. The British is still going along with this. On the 1st of October, Eden's Foreign Secretary Selwyn Lloyd, who will talk more about next time, he flies to New York for talks at the UN.

174
01:19:18.000 --> 01:19:40.000
Lloyd is actually deep down hoping that the talks will work and we can avoid military actually. It's not a hawk at all. And after 12 days, it seems like they've got somewhere. The Egyptians agree, they promise that people, they'll keep the canal open to ships of all countries, whatever their politics. And Lloyd is delighted. He reports to London and he says, great news, we've got what we wanted.

175
01:19:40.000 --> 01:20:01.000
I think this could be the end of the crisis, actually. And Eden says, oh, brilliant. Two days later, 14th of October. Eden says Selwyn, I'd like you to organise some more talks. Maybe we can meet in Geneva. We can hammer out all the details of this and then it's a done deal. But Eden is not being honest with his own foreign secretary because that very afternoon at his country house checkers,

176
01:20:01.000 --> 01:20:26.000
he has a visit from two Frenchmen who arrive in total secrecy. One of them is the French Labour Minister, who's a man called Albert Gazier, and the other is the deputy chief of the French general staff, who is General Maurice Schall. And they are shown in to see Anthony Eden. Anthony Eden is with his junior Foreign Office Minister, Anthony Notting, and his private secretary, who's called Guy Millard.

177
01:20:26.000 --> 01:21:03.000
And when the Frenchman come in, Eden says quite casually, there's no need to take any notes of this guy. And at that moment, this broke nutting thinks, oh, this is not good. So in the next few hours, the French unveil their plan. They've got an offer for Eden. And the French say, look, we are absolutely determined now to get rid of NASA. We have been tracking this gigantic mind-sweeping ship called the Athos, which left Alexandria a few. days ago and it's been loaded with 70 tons of military equipment, mortars, machine guns, automatic

178
01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:38.000
rifles and arsenal for 3,000 men. And these are destined for the guerrillas in Algeria. We know that they were bought with Egyptian money and they were loaded aboard by uniformed Egyptian soldiers. And so we have made up our minds NASA's got to go. We are set on regime change and you know what? We have been talking to our friends, the Israelis, because France and Israel are very close allies at this point. The French are in fact Israel's chief arms suppliers. Now the Israelis can't stand NASA.

179
01:21:38.000 --> 01:22:15.000
They've already fought and won one war against Egypt, 1948, 49. Egypt's fleet is blockading the Israeli port of Islat, and they blame NASA for encouraging attacks by Palestinian guerrillas from Gaza on Israel. And Nasser, in fact, has been very explicit about his desire to wipe Israel off the map. He told a crowd in August 195, my ambition is to cleanse the land of Palestine. We demand vengeance, and vengeance is Israel's death. The Israelis are also very worried about NASA's Czech arms deal, of course.

180
01:22:15.000 --> 01:22:55.000
So their military chief, Moshe Daya, said afterwards there was no doubt in our minds that Egypt's purpose was to wipe. us out. He's the guy with the eyepatch. He's the guy with the eyepatch, exactly. Big figure in sort of 1950s' newspapers. So between them, the French and the Israelis have come up with a plan. The French will provide Israel with a massive new injection of arms. They'll send them 100 tanks and 200 armored personnel carriers. Israel will launch a massive attack across the Sinai Desert striking towards the canal. And meanwhile, on the pretext of safeguarding the international passage through the canal. French paratroopers will land west of the canal.

181
01:22:55.000 --> 01:23:31.000
They will take it over, and then they will head west towards Cairo to deal with NASA. It's a massive gamble, but yeah, the French have tremendous confidence in their military prowess. They reckon they can pull it off and they say to Eden, listen, if you join us, this will be a piece of cake. And they say to him, this is our chance. We can win back the canal. We can bring down NASA. and we can stand up for the great empires of Britain and France against these insolent Arab and North African pygmies. So, Sir Anthony, are you in?

182
01:23:31.000 --> 01:24:04.000
Goodness, what a choice and what a cliffhanger. And we will find out Eden's answer. I'm sure it'll come as a complete surprise to all our listeners. Next time, and discover what the consequences are for Britain, for America, for France, for the Middle East. and Restless History Club members, of course, can hear that right now. And if you're not a member of the Rested History Club, then go to The Restedithistry.com and you can sign up there. And when you do, you'll be able to hear two episodes on the Hungarian Revolution,

